<p>
  Zippiker keeps <strong>double-entry books</strong>
  for you. You don't need to be an accountant: most
  entries are written automatically when you invoice and get paid. You only record the money that
  goes out, and anything unusual.
</p>

<h2>Setting up</h2>
<p>
  The first time you open <.link navigate="/accounting">Accounting</.link>, an admin clicks <strong>Set up accounting</strong>. Zippiker creates a standard chart of accounts and posts every
  invoice and payment you've already recorded, so your books start complete.
</p>

<h2>What happens automatically</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>When you…</th><th>Your books record</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Issue an invoice</td>
      <td>
        The customer owes you the total (<em>Accounts receivable</em>), you earned the net amount
        (<em>Sales revenue</em>) and you owe the tax (<em>Sales tax payable</em>).
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Record a payment</td>
      <td>
        Money arrives in <em>Bank</em>, <em>Cash</em>
        or <em>Mobile money</em>
        (depending on the method) and
        the customer owes that much less.
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Receive goods on a purchase order</td>
      <td>
        Your <em>Inventory</em>
        grows, and <em>Goods received not billed</em>
        holds what you'll owe
        until the supplier's bill arrives.
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Approve a bill</td>
      <td>
        You owe the supplier (<em>Accounts payable</em>). Goods from a purchase order clear <em>Goods received not billed</em>; other stock goes to <em>Inventory</em>; services go to the
        expense account on each line. Tax you paid goes to <em>Input tax receivable</em>.
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Pay a bill</td>
      <td>
        Money leaves the bank (or cash, mobile money) and you owe the supplier that much less.
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Deliver goods</td>
      <td>
        What they cost you moves from <em>Inventory</em>
        to <em>Cost of sales</em>, so your profit is
        sales minus the cost of what you sold.
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Adjust stock</td>
      <td>
        The difference goes to <em>Inventory adjustments</em>
        (a loss, or a gain when you find more).
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Void an invoice or bill, remove a payment</td>
      <td>The original entry is reversed. Nothing is deleted, so the history stays readable.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Recording expenses</h2>
<p>
  For quick out-of-pocket spending, click <strong>+ Expense</strong>
  on the accounting overview. (When a
  supplier sends an invoice you'll pay later, enter it as a
  <.link navigate="/help/purchasing">bill</.link>
  instead.) Say what it was for, the amount,
  the category (rent, software, travel…) and where the money came from (bank, cash, mobile money or the
  company credit card). Optionally pick the supplier.
</p>

<h2>Journal entries</h2>
<p>
  For everything else (the owner putting money in, a loan, buying equipment, a correction), an admin
  can post a <strong>journal entry</strong>
  under <.link navigate="/accounting/journal">Journal → + Journal entry</.link>.
  Each line goes to one account as either a debit or a credit, and the totals must match: the
  form tells you when it's balanced.
</p>
<.callout>
  Debits and credits in one sentence: <strong>assets and expenses go up with debits; liabilities,
  equity and income go up with credits.</strong> Owner puts 5,000 in the bank? Debit Bank 5,000,
  credit Owner's equity 5,000.
</.callout>
<p>
  Entries are never edited. If one is wrong, open it and click <strong>Reverse</strong>: Zippiker posts
  the mirror image dated today, then you post the correct one. Automatic entries are corrected from
  their document instead (void the invoice or remove the payment).
</p>

<h2>Chart of accounts</h2>
<p>
  <.link navigate="/accounting/accounts">Chart of accounts</.link>
  lists every account with its balance.
  Click one to see its ledger: every posting with a running balance. Admins can add accounts (say <em>6150 Insurance</em>), rename or renumber them, and deactivate the ones they don't use. Accounts
  marked <strong>Auto</strong>
  are used by invoices and payments and can't be deleted.
</p>

<h2>Financial statements</h2>
<p>
  The <strong>Statements</strong>
  tab shows the three core statements. Many more (cash flows, general ledger, journal,
  adjusted trial balance…) are in <.link navigate="/help/reports">Reports</.link>.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Profit &amp; loss</strong>
    — income minus expenses for any period. Are you making money?
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Balance sheet</strong>
    — what you own (assets), what you owe (liabilities) and what's left for
    the owners (equity, including profit to date) on any date. It always balances.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Trial balance</strong>
    — every account's debit or credit balance; the two totals match.
    Your accountant will ask for this one.
  </li>
</ul>
<p>Every report can be printed or saved as PDF.</p>
<.callout kind={:warning}>
  Zippiker gives you a solid, always-current set of books. Your accountant should still review
  them before you file taxes or close a financial year.
</.callout>
